Z||000000||moonie||08-10-2001||06:02 PM||||my battery slid over on the water pump <BR>pulley. it leaks a tiny amount from the <BR>rubbed place.<BR>is there anything i canput on there <BR>to stop the leak ?<BR>maybe J B Weld or something like that.<P>thanks  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-10-2001||06:08 PM||||nope the acid will eat it, also anything under it like battery tray, wiring, etc. replace it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-10-2001||06:33 PM||||replace it. preferably today. the leaking cell won't last long. It can do plenty of damage on the way out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||maryjane||08-10-2001||06:34 PM||||Roger's right. That's the easy part. Don't forget to rinse off anywhere and everwhere the acid may have leaked to or  been thrown by the belt. It may take a while to show up but I shudder to think what the eng compartment can look like in a couple months if you don't get it cleaned. Pay special attention to soft metals like alum hose connections.||||reg||4
Z||000004||rockcrawl||08-10-2001||08:49 PM||||Well, I know I may get flamed for this, but.... I have repaired a few batteries because of the exact problem you have and two of them were from Fieros. The first one was about three years ago and it's still working. I just plug the vent in the cap and set the battery on it's side. Find a piece of the same kind of plastic to use as filler. I use the handle that comes on some batteries and cut it into "welding rods". Heat up a big soldering iron and go to town welding up the hole. Run a small torch over it when your done to smooth out the seam. The one I did about a week ago was on a V8 Fiero and had a 1/8 wide cut about 5 inches long from the serp belt. If you do it right, it won't leak and will hardly be noticeable. In fact my buddy couldn't believe it was the same battery. Fill up the end cell with electrolyte and your back in business. I know it sounds crazy, but I'm a cheap bastard. I'll try to fix anything.<P>I'll take some pics of the latest repair and post them up here.||||reg||1
Z||000005||moonie||08-10-2001||09:54 PM||||LOL<BR>i'm a cheapskate too & the battery is only about 3 months old, so ihate to just toss it.<BR>i'll try the handle melting deal.<P>thanks <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||08-10-2001||10:35 PM||||I <B>strongly</B> advise <B>NOT</B> using rockcrawls method!<P>Many years ago a friend of mine was using exactly that procedure in his shop. I started out watching from WAY across the shop because it just didn't sound like a real good idea to me. Since nothing bad was happening I slowly got closer. When I got about 20 feet away the battery exploded and blew the whole top off. It just happened to be leaned toward me so I wound up getting splattered with acid. I went outside and flushed my eyes with water for 10 or 20 minutes. It was leaned away from the the guy doing the "welding" so he didn't get anything except a ringing in his ears for a little bit. No real damage to me, but I can't say the same for the clothes I had on.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>It could have been a <B>LOT</B> worse. <P>Not meant as a flame rockcrawl, just a different perspective...<P>||||reg||13
Z||000007||starfighter007@msn.com||08-10-2001||10:45 PM||||I know someone in the body shop repair and buissness.And broken and cracked batterys are very common, any way they  use a hot glue gun to repair the batteries .I guess you would have to make sure the spot you are repairing is very clean as with anything that you have to glue.||||reg||3
Z||000008||Primaris||08-11-2001||12:36 AM||||If you want to try and fix the hole go for it, but DO NOT USE FLAME near a battery!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> Hydrogen and flame mix well!  Boom!<P>------------------<BR>Signatures are a waste of bandwidth!||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||08-11-2001||06:33 AM||||amen, I know my eyes are worth more than $40 to me.||||reg||1
Z||000010||rockcrawl||08-11-2001||01:33 PM||||You guys have a valid point about the explosiveness of batteries. I work in the material handling industry (electric forklift mechanic). I have seen the Deka battery technicians repair industrial batteries on several occasions. I'm talking about batteries that weigh 3000 pounds and cost $3000. These batteries create a lot of explosive gas. They have lead bars connecting the individual cells on the top. When a single cell goes bad, the only way to replace it is to cut the lead bar off and solder a new one on. They use a torch to do this. They first blow compressed air into the cell to blow out any hydrogen. Then they fire up the acetylene torch and put the flame <B>directly into the cell</B> to burn out any remaining hydrogen gas. Then they use a pot of molten lead to solder the new bar on and use the torch to smooth out the top of the lead joint. The first time I saw this done I almost ran the other way, but after talking to the battery tech and learning what he was doing, it's not so scary. These guys have no fear of sparking up a torch while standing over a battery. I guess I should add to my method, blowing air into the cells to disperse hydrogen gas, and leave the caps off while working on it.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||08-11-2001||02:11 PM||||You know, you COULD just drain the battery into a large GLASS container, let the case dry for a couple of hours, THEN plastic-weld the patch...||||reg||6
Z||000012||moonie||08-11-2001||04:50 PM||||i removed the battery.<BR>carried it into the kitchen & propped it<BR>up on its side.<BR>waited about 30 minutes for the side to dry & then used screwdrivers heated up on the stove to melt the handle into the grinded out place. smoothed everything out with a hot screwdriver & sat the battery outside on the woodpile.<BR>no moisture , no leak , nothing.<BR>you have to look to even see the patched place.<P>thanks for the idea & responses.<BR>saved me 40 bucks.<P>moonie  <P>||||reg||1
